    'The Lord of the Healers and the Stone Jewel of the Gomti', 
    'Baijnath Temple | Uttarakhand, Shaiva & Ancient Lore', 
    'Experience the profound healing of Baijnath. Discover the cluster of ancient temples, the legend of the Divine Physician, and the profound energy of the Kumaon pilgrimage.', 
    'Baijnath, Uttarakhand, Shiva, Kumaon, Ancient Temples, Hindu Pilgrimage, Ancient Lore, Bageshwar, Healing', 
    '212', 
    '{
        "spiritualEssence": "Baijnath is the manifestation of the divine as the supreme physician and the absolute restorer of the life-force. The energy here is calm, aquatic, and intensely curative. It is the site where the Lord personally practiced the arts of healing. The vibration is one of ''Aarogya'' (Health) and the absolute removal of all suffering. As a complex of dozens of stone temples reflected in the clear waters of the Gomti, it represents the spiritual pharmacy of the Himalayas. A visit here is believed to grant the devotee the absolute recovery from all physical and mental diseases and the blessing of the divine vitality. The air is always vibrant with the scent of the mountain herbs and the silent, heavy energy of the 1,000-year-old stone shikharas.",
        "longDescription": "Baijnath was the capital of the Katyuri dynasty of Uttarakhand. The main temple is dedicated to Shiva as Vaidyanath (Lord of the Healers). The site features a spectacular cluster of 18 temples built in the 12th century, featuring exquisite stone carvings. A unique feature is the beautiful idol of Goddess Parvati carved in black stone inside the main sanctum, which is considered one of the most beautiful images in the Himalayas. The temple complex is situated in the fertile Katyur valley and is famous for its large pond full of sacred fish (Mahseer) which are fed by the pilgrims. Baijnath is a site where the history of the Kumaon kingdoms and the ancient traditions of Himalayan medicine are perfectly unified.",
        "spiritualArchitecture": "The architecture of Baijnath is a spectacular display of the Katyuri style, characterized by its high, tiered shikharas and the use of the massive gray stone blocks. The temples are arranged in a precise cluster on a raised platform by the river. A unique feature is the use of the relief carvings of the various deities on the exterior walls, which show a high level of artistic detail. The architecture is designed to lead the pilgrim from the river ghats to the central sanctum, creating a sense of a spiritual city of the healers. The use of the stone-cut steps leading into the river and the expansive courtyard create a sense of a spiritual retreat that is both grounded and divine.",
        "vedicReferences": "Baijnath is celebrated in the local Puranic literature as the site where the Lord Shiva personally revealed the secrets of the Ayurveda to the sages.",
        "deepInsights": "The presence of the Divine Physician represents the truth that the highest healing is the alignment of the individual soul with the cosmic order. Baijnath teaches that the divine is the source of all vitality.",
        "ancientLore": "Lore tells that the temple was built in a single night by the heavenly architects. Another legend says that the sacred fish in the Gomti river are the descendants of the celestial beings who took the form of fish to listen to the Lord''s discourses.",
        "keyRituals": [
                {
                        "name": "Vaidyanath Abhishekam",
                        "description": "The ritual bath of the Divine Physician with sacred oils and herb-infused waters to seek healing."
                },
                {
                        "name": "Fish Feeding (Gomti)",
                        "description": "The unique tradition of feeding the sacred Mahseer fish in the temple pond as an act of compassion and merit-making."
                },
                {
                        "name": "Parvati Shringaar",
                        "description": "The elaborate ritual of decorating the beautiful black stone idol of the Goddess with silk and jewelry."
                },
                {
                        "name": "Maha Shivratri (Baijnath)",
                        "description": "The grand annual celebration of the Lord of the Healers, attracting thousands of pilgrims from across Kumaon."
                }
        ],
        "highlights": [
                {
                        "name": "The Vaidyanath Temple",
                        "description": "The central stone structure dedicated to the Lord of the Healers."
                },
                {
                        "name": "The Black Stone Parvati",
                        "description": "One of the most beautiful and well-preserved medieval sculptures in India."
                },
                {
                        "name": "The Gomti River Ghats",
                        "description": "The beautiful stone steps leading to the river that flows past the temple."
                },
                {
                        "name": "The Sacred Fish Pond",
                        "description": "The ancient tank full of large and friendly Mahseer fish that are protected by the temple."
                }
        ],
        "travelInfo": {
                "bestTime": "October to April.",
                "howToReach": "17km from Kausani and 70km from Almora. Well connected by road; regular buses and taxis run from Almora and Kathgodam.",
                "nearestAirport": "Pantnagar Airport.",
                "nearestRailway": "Kathgodam Railway Station."
        },
        "tips": [
                "Carry some parched grain or gram to feed the sacred fish in the river; it is a deeply joyful local tradition.",
                "Visit the nearby Kausani for a panoramic view of the Himalayan peaks after your spiritual darshan at Baijnath.",
                "The temple is very peaceful and well-maintained; it is an ideal spot for quiet meditation by the river."
        ],
        "faqs": [
                {
                        "question": "Who were the Katyuri kings?",
                        "answer": "They were the rulers of a medieval kingdom that controlled most of Uttarakhand and parts of Western Nepal between the 7th and 13th centuries."
                },
                {
                        "question": "What is unique about the Parvati idol?",
                        "answer": "It is carved from a single piece of dark black stone and is incredibly detailed and lifelike, considered a masterpiece of the era."
