    '219', 
    '{
        "spiritualEssence": "Dhari Devi is the manifestation of the divine as the supreme guardian and the absolute protector of the spiritual ecology. The energy here is sharp, alert, and intensely territorial. It is the site where the mother keeps watch over the entry to the higher Himalayas. The vibration is one of ''Raksha'' (Protection) and the absolute balance between nature and human presence. As a temple that has been recently raised on pillars to stay above the reservoir of the Alaknanda, it represents the resilience of the ancient faith in the face of modern development. A visit here is believed to grant the devotee the absolute safe passage through the mountains and the blessing of the divine guardian. The air is always vibrant with the scent of the river mist and the constant, thunderous roar of the Alaknanda.",
        "longDescription": "The Dhari Devi temple is situated in the Garhwal region. Legend tells that the Goddess is the guardian deity of the Char Dham pilgrimage; her head is at Dhari Devi and her lower body is at Kalimath. She is believed to change her appearance from a girl in the morning to a young woman in the afternoon and an old woman in the evening. The temple became world-famous after the 2013 Kedarnath floods; locals believe the disaster occurred because the original temple was removed from its spot just hours before the flood. Today, the Goddess is housed in a modern structure built on high pillars above the water. Dhari Devi is a site where the raw power of the river and the deep mysticism of the mountain guardianship are perfectly unified in a single stone idol.",
        "spiritualArchitecture": "The architecture of Dhari Devi is a spectacular display of the modern engineering serving the ancient faith. The temple is built on massive concrete pillars rising from the center of the Alaknanda reservoir. A unique feature is the open-air sanctum that allows the Goddess to remain under the open sky, according to the traditional requirement. The architecture is designed to allow the pilgrims to walk across a long bridge from the highway to the center of the river. The use of the vibrant red colors and the sound of the water flowing directly beneath the temple create a sense of a spiritual island that is both part of the mountain and the river. The complex includes several meditation platforms that offer a direct view of the mountain ranges.",
        "vedicReferences": "Dhari Devi is celebrated in the local Puranic literature as the supreme force that maintains the stability of the Himalayan peaks and prevents the floods.",
        "deepInsights": "The changing appearance of the Goddess represents the truth that the divine is a living presence that moves through the cycles of time. Dhari Devi teaches that the highest respect must be paid to the original anchors of the sacred land.",
        "ancientLore": "Lore tells that the Goddess personally commanded the ancient kings to never build a roof over her head so that she could always watch the heavens. Another legend says that the roar of the Alaknanda changes its tone to warn the temple priest of any impending mountain disaster.",
        "keyRituals": [
