    'Triyuginarayan Temple | Uttarakhand, Shiva-Parvati Wedding & Ancient Lore', 
    'Experience the profound union of Triyuginarayan. Discover the site of the divine marriage, the eternal fire, and the profound energy of the wedding pilgrimage.', 
    'Triyuginarayan, Uttarakhand, Shiva, Parvati, Wedding, Eternal Fire, Hindu Pilgrimage, Ancient Lore, Marriage', 
    '225', 
    '{
        "spiritualEssence": "Triyuginarayan is the manifestation of the divine as the supreme union and the absolute commitment of the cosmic creative forces. The energy here is warm, festive, and intensely auspicious. It is the site where the masculine and the feminine aspects of the universe were anchored in a sacred bond. The vibration is one of ''Mangalyam'' (Auspiciousness) and the absolute harmony of the dualities. As a stone temple perched on a high ridge overlooking the Kedarnath valley, it represents the spiritual heart of the Himalayan family. A visit here is believed to grant the devotee the absolute stability of the relationship and the blessing of a long and prosperous marriage. The air is always vibrant with the scent of the wood-fire and the constant, rhythmic chanting of the wedding hymns.",
        "longDescription": "The Triyuginarayan temple is modeled after the Kedarnath temple architecture. Legend tells that Lord Vishnu acted as the brother of Parvati and Lord Brahma as the priest for the wedding of Shiva and Parvati. The Akhand Dhuni (perpetual flame) in the temple courtyard is believed to have been burning since the wedding occurred, three Yugas (ages) ago. Pilgrims offer wood to the fire and take the sacred ash (Vibhuti) as a blessing. The site also features four sacred ponds: Brahma Kund, Vishnu Kund, Rudra Kund, and Saraswati Kund, where the various gods performed their ablutions before the ceremony. Triyuginarayan has become a premier destination for spiritual weddings, where couples seek to anchor their own vows in the presence of the cosmic altar. It is a site where the raw elements of fire and the highest levels of Vedic ritual are perfectly unified.",
        "spiritualArchitecture": "The architecture of Triyuginarayan is a spectacular display of the Himalayan Nagara style. The temple is built of massive gray stone slabs, featuring a high tiered shikhara and a small mandapam. A unique feature is the central fire-altar (Dhuni) in the courtyard, which is the primary focus of the pilgrimage. The architecture is designed to lead the pilgrim through the four sacred kunds before entering the intimate sanctum. The use of the ancient stone and the simple, balanced proportions create a sense of a spiritual home that is as eternal as the fire it houses. The courtyard includes several smaller shrines dedicated to the gods who attended the divine wedding.",
        "vedicReferences": "Triyuginarayan is celebrated in the Puranas as the supreme site where the cosmic balance was restored through the union of the Lord and the Mother.",
        "deepInsights": "The eternal fire represents the truth that the highest commitment is self-sustaining and transcends the limitations of time. Triyuginarayan teaches that the ultimate union is the integration of the soul with the divine.",
        "ancientLore": "Lore tells that the wood for the eternal fire is personally protected by the forest spirits of the mountain. Another legend says that those who marry in this temple are blessed with the same eternal love that exists between Shiva and Parvati.",
        "keyRituals": [
                {
                        "name": "Akhand Dhuni Seva",
                        "description": "Offering sacred wood to the eternal fire to seek the blessing of the cosmic union."
                },
                {
                        "name": "Brahma-Vishnu Kund Snanam",
                        "description": "The ritual dip in the sacred ponds to purify the spirit before the darshan."
                },
                {
                        "name": "Spiritual Wedding (Vivah)",
                        "description": "The performance of the marriage ceremony at the same altar where the gods were unified."
                },
                {
                        "name": "Vibhuti Tilak",
                        "description": "The ritual of applying the sacred ash from the eternal fire to the forehead to seek protection."
                }
        ],
        "highlights": [
                {
                        "name": "The Akhand Dhuni",
                        "description": "The perpetual fire that has burned for three Yugas, the primary miracle of the temple."
                },
                {
                        "name": "The Wedding Altar",
                        "description": "The sacred spot where Shiva and Parvati took their seven steps."
                },
                {
                        "name": "The Four Sacred Kunds",
                        "description": "The ancient ponds associated with the various gods who attended the wedding."
                },
                {
                        "name": "The Kedarnath-Style Shikhara",
                        "description": "The beautiful stone spire that mirrors the architecture of the high mountain shrine."
                }
        ],
        "travelInfo": {
                "bestTime": "April to June and September to November (the village is also beautiful in winter when the snow falls).",
                "howToReach": "Reached by a 5km motorable road from Sonprayag. Well connected by road; regular taxis and buses run from Rishikesh and Rudraprayag.",
                "nearestAirport": "Jolly Grant Airport, Dehradun.",
                "nearestRailway": "Rishikesh Railway Station."
        },
        "tips": [
                "Carry some dry wood or Ghee to offer to the Akhand Dhuni; it is the most sacred act in this temple.",
                "Take a small amount of the sacred ash (Vibhuti) from the fire back with you for protection and blessing.",
                "If you are planning a wedding here, make sure to coordinate with the local priests and the temple committee well in advance."
        ],
        "faqs": [
                {
