    true, 
    true, 
    'The Sky-High Shakti of the Garhwal Ridges and the Crown of the Himalayas', 
    'Surkanda Devi Temple | Uttarakhand, Shakti Peeth & Ancient Lore', 
    'Experience the profound energy of Surkanda Devi. Discover the ridgetop Shakti Peeth, the legend of the falling head of Sati, and the profound energy of the mountain pilgrimage.', 
    'Surkanda Devi, Uttarakhand, Shakti Peeth, Dhanaulti, Hindu Pilgrimage, Ancient Lore, High Altitude, Mussoorie', 
    '242', 
    '{
        "spiritualEssence": "Surkanda Devi is the manifestation of the divine as the supreme elevation and the absolute clarity of the mountain-top presence. The energy here is expansive, thin, and intensely luminous. It is the site where the highest point of the ridge becomes the altar of the Mother. The vibration is one of ''Uchcha'' (Elevation) and the absolute perspective over the world of the forms. As a temple that offers a 360-degree view of the high snow peaks and the deep valleys of the Doon, it represents the spiritual crown of the central Garhwal. A visit here is believed to grant the devotee the absolute lifting of the spirits and the blessing of the divine vision. The air is always vibrant with the scent of the rhododendrons and the constant, rhythmic sound of the wind through the high-altitude deodars.",
        "longDescription": "Surkanda Devi is one of the 51 Shakti Peethas. According to the Puranas, when Lord Shiva was carrying the charred body of Sati, her head fell at this spot. The temple is famous for its location, requiring a steep 2km trek from the village of Kaddukhal. It is a site of intense local devotion, especially during the Ganga Dashahara festival when a large fair is held. The temple structure is modern but the energy is ancient, rooted in the primitive rock worship of the Himalayas. The site provides a direct spiritual link to the nearby shrines of Kunjapuri and Chandrabadni, forming a powerful triangle of Shakti that protects the region. It is a site where the highest Himalayan weather and the most resilient local faith are perfectly unified.",
        "spiritualArchitecture": "The architecture of Surkanda Devi is a spectacular display of the modern mountain temple style with a focus on durability and verticality. The temple features a prominent white dome and a series of paved courtyards that wrap around the ridge peak. A unique feature is the presence of several open-air viewpoints where pilgrims sit in silence to face the Nanda Devi and Trishul peaks. The architecture is designed to emphasize the exposure to the elements and the isolation of the peak, creating a sense of a spiritual fortress that is suspended in the sky. The use of the local stone and the simple white-washed facade create a sense of a spiritual home that is visible for miles across the surrounding mountains.",
        "vedicReferences": "Surkanda Devi is celebrated in the Skanda Purana as the supreme site where the divine head of the Mother was anchored to guide the destiny of the Northern lands.",
        "deepInsights": "The falling of the head represents the truth that the highest intellect must be surrendered to the divine will. Surkanda Devi teaches that the highest perspective is reached only after a difficult climb.",
        "ancientLore": "Lore tells that the mountain peak personally rose higher to receive the sacred relic of the Mother. Another legend says that the fog that often covers the temple is the subtle veil of the Goddess, protecting the secret rituals of the mountain devas.",
        "keyRituals": [
                {
                        "name": "Ganga Dashahara Mela",
                        "description": "Participating in the massive annual fair to seek the blessing of the Mother during the descent of the Ganga."
                },
                {
                        "name": "Surkanda Peak Japa",
                        "description": "Performing the repetitive chanting of the Devi mantras while facing the massive snow peaks at sunrise."
                },
                {
                        "name": "Kaddukhal Trek Pilgrimage",
                        "description": "The ritual of the climb as a form of physical and spiritual penance to reach the Mother''s feet."
                },
                {
                        "name": "Ridge-Top Deep Daan",
                        "description": "Offering lamps at the highest point of the temple to seek the blessing of the inner light."
                }
        ],
        "highlights": [
                {
                        "name": "The Main Shrine",
                        "description": "The sacred heart of the ridge where the energy of the Shakti Peeth is most intensely felt."
                },
                {
                        "name": "The 360-Degree Viewpoint",
                        "description": "The spectacular perspective of the entire Himalayan range and the Doon valley."
                },
                {
                        "name": "The Ancient Rock Altar",
                        "description": "The foundation of the temple believed to be the specific spot where the head of Sati fell."
                },
                {
                        "name": "The Deodar Forest Trail",
                        "description": "The beautiful and sacred path through the high-altitude forests that lead to the temple."
                }
        ],
        "travelInfo": {
                "bestTime": "April to June and September to November (the trek is beautiful during the spring and autumn).",
                "howToReach": "8km from Dhanaulti and 35km from Mussoorie. Reached by road to Kaddukhal, followed by a 2km uphill trek (ponies available).",
                "nearestAirport": "Jolly Grant Airport, Dehradun.",
                "nearestRailway": "Dehradun Railway Station."
        },
        "tips": [
                "Start the trek early in the morning to catch the sunrise over the snow peaks; it is a life-changing spiritual experience.",
                "The weather can change rapidly at this altitude; carry a light jacket and rain protection even in the summer.",
                "Respect the silence of the inner sanctum; the temple is a site of deep meditation for many local practitioners."
        ],
        "faqs": [
                {
                        "question": "How difficult is the trek?",
                        "answer": "It is a 2km steep climb on a paved path. It takes about 45-60 minutes for a person with average fitness."
                },
                {
                        "question": "Is it a Shakti Peeth?",
                        "answer": "Yes, it is one of the 51 Shakti Peethas, where the head of Sati is said to have fallen."
                },
                {
