        "spiritualEssence": "Devidhura is the manifestation of the divine as the supreme vitality and the absolute power of the folk-will. The energy here is raw, intense, and intensely grounded. It is the site where the blood and the stone meet the spiritual devotion. The vibration is one of ''Veera'' (Heroism) and the absolute protection of the local community. As a temple located in a high-altitude meadow surrounded by massive boulders and oak forests, it represents the spiritual heart of the frontier Kumaon. A visit here is believed to grant the devotee the absolute courage and the blessing of the physical and spiritual strength. The air is always vibrant with the silent, heavy energy of the ancient battles and the constant, rhythmic sound of the mountain wind through the rocks.",
        "longDescription": "Devidhura is famous for the Maa Barahi Devi temple and the unique Bagwal festival held on Raksha Bandhan. During this festival, four local clans (Khams) engage in a symbolic and sometimes real stone-pelting battle until the Goddess is satisfied by the offering of human blood. In recent years, fruit and flowers have been introduced to replace the stones by the court order, but the spirit of the ritual remains. The temple is situated in a natural rock clearing and features two massive boulders between which the image of the Goddess is kept. The site is a critical center for the Kumaoni identity and attracts thousands of visitors for the annual fair. Devidhura is a site where the highest level of Puranic Shakti worship and the most ancient tribal warrior traditions are perfectly unified.",
        "spiritualArchitecture": "The architecture of Devidhura is a spectacular display of the megalithic-spiritual style using the massive natural boulders as the primary structure. The temple features a simple stone roof built between two enormous rocks. A unique feature is the presence of the wide, natural arena (Dubichaur) where the Bagwal festival takes place. The architecture is designed to emphasize the connection to the primitive and the indestructible nature of the earth. The use of the raw rock and the simple stone carvings create a sense of a spiritual home that is not built by hands but found in the landscape. The complex includes several smaller shrines and a series of ancient stone platforms for the community gatherings.",
